%NETWORK
// a$"b
ASSIGN(a, Q => x);
=== refused
network-plain-st the escape '$"'

#### execute-without-code — the Execute box takes EN := where it shows EN, then its ST, and nothing else
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
EXECUTE(EN := a);
=== refused
network-plain-st the Execute box takes EN := first where it shows EN, then its ST as one string '<ST>'

#### execute-code-pin — the Execute box's ST is one positional string, not a pin
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
EXECUTE(EN := a, CODE := 'k := 1;');
=== refused
network-plain-st the Execute box takes EN := first where it shows EN, then its ST as one string '<ST>'

#### execute-declared-in-reach — EXECUTE is the text's Execute box: a POU of that name in reach would give it two meanings
=== scope
pou EXECUTE
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
EXECUTE(EN := a, 'k := 1;');
=== refused
network-plain-st which the text reads as its Execute box

#### execute-code-escape — the code spells a ' as $' and a $ as $$, and everything else as it stands
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
EXECUTE('s := $Nb;');
=== refused
network-plain-st the escape '$N' in the Execute box's ST

#### string-across-a-line — only the Execute box's ST spans lines
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
ASSIGN(MOVE('a
b'), Q => x);
=== refused
network-plain-st a string across a line

#### execute-beside-a-variable-named-execute — EXECUTE is the text's own word: anything declared in reach under it is refused, a variable too
=== scope
name Execute
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
EXECUTE(EN := a, 'k := 1;');
=== refused
network-plain-st which the text reads as its Execute box

#### execute-code-unclosed — the Execute box's ST not closed runs to the body's end and is refused there
=== text
