%NETWORK
EXECUTE('k := 1;', EN := a);
=== refused
network-plain-st the Execute box takes EN := first where it shows EN, then its ST as one string '<ST>'

#### execute-eno-pin — the Execute box's ENO is its one output, read where it stands: no ENO => v pin
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
EXECUTE(EN := a, 'k := 1;', ENO => v);
=== refused
network-plain-st the Execute box takes EN := first where it shows EN, then its ST as one string '<ST>'

#### execute-code-wstring — the Execute box's ST is a '…' string, never a "…" one
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
EXECUTE(EN := a, "k := 1;");
=== refused
network-plain-st the Execute box takes EN := first where it shows EN, then its ST as one string '<ST>'

#### execute-code-wstring-across-a-line — a "…" string spans no line, in the Execute box either
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
EXECUTE(EN := a, "k := 1;
n := 2;");
=== refused
network-plain-st a string across a line

#### code-string-of-another-call — a string pin of a call other than the Execute box spans no line
=== scope
pou f
=== text
%LANGUAGE LD
%NETWORK
ASSIGN(f(CODE := 'a
b'), Q => x);
=== refused
network-plain-st a string across a line

#### a-box-named-execute — a call headed Execute is the Execute box, in any case: a box of that type has no other spelling
=== text
%LANGUAGE FBD
%NETWORK
ASSIGN(Execute(IN := b), Q => x);
=== refused
network-plain-st the Execute box takes EN := first where it shows EN, then its ST as one string '<ST>'

